  "notes": [
    {
      "title": "Certificates and master contracts have different roles",
      "text": "A certificate describes the insured member’s protection; the group policyholder holds the master contract. Individual life ordinarily uses an individual policy, while group life connects coverage to an eligible group and its terms.",
      "example": "An employee with a certificate does not thereby own the employer’s master contract.",
      "topics": [
        "4.5.01",
        "3.6.01"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recognize the qualifying group",
      "text": "Maryland separately authorizes employer, labor-union, trust, association and debtor arrangements. Under §17-203, qualifying trusts can involve multiple employers, unions, or their permitted combinations. Under §17-204, an association must initially have at least 100 people, two years of active existence, a genuine purpose other than obtaining insurance, and the specified governance, meeting and dues/contribution conditions.",
      "example": "A newly created club of ten applicants formed solely to purchase coverage does not meet that association provision.",
      "topics": [
        "4.5.02",
        "4.5.03",
        "4.5.04",
        "4.5.05",
        "4.5.06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Benefit payment includes specific exceptions",
      "text": "The ordinary group provision in §17-307 pays the insured’s designated beneficiary, subject to the policy’s survival and permitted expense-payment provisions. A reserved certificate-disclosed right can allow up to $2,500 to an equitably entitled person for funeral or last-illness expenses. Creditor policies have a different scope under §17-301 and their debt-related rules.",
      "example": "Do not assume the master policyholder receives every group death benefit.",
      "topics": [
        "4.5.08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Dependent coverage is separately defined",
      "text": "Under the covered Maryland arrangements in §17-209, dependent protection may include a spouse, domestic partner or eligible dependent child. The dependent amount cannot exceed the employee/member amount. The statute’s child definition is not a universal age-26 rule. See Part 16 for the statutory details.",
      "example": "Naming a spouse as the employee’s beneficiary does not enroll that spouse for a separate death benefit.",
      "topics": [
        "4.5.09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Contributions do not establish one universal enrollment percentage",
      "text": "Employee premium participation makes a plan contributory. For the Maryland employer noncontributory rule, apply coverage of eligible employees with the written-rejection and insurability exceptions. Do not import a generic 75% contributory rule when the question has not supplied it and the applicable statute does not impose it.",
      "example": "A question explicitly giving a plan participation requirement should be answered using that stated requirement.",
      "topics": [
        "4.5.07",
        "4.5.11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Different conversion events have different rules",
      "text": "Ordinary employment or eligible membership termination, termination of the group policy/class, late conversion notice and death during the conversion opportunity are separate events. Part 16 teaches §§17-308 through 17-311. Portability is an available continuation feature when offered; it is not a synonym for every statutory individual conversion.",
      "example": "A group policy terminating after six years and a person leaving a continuing employer plan are different fact patterns.",
      "topics": [
        "4.5.10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use current federal eligibility guidance",
      "text": "FEGLI is group term with Basic and optional coverage. SGLI serves eligible service members. VA’s current VGLI guidance generally requires application within one year and 120 days after separation; applying within 240 days avoids the health-evidence requirement. These time periods have different purposes and remain subject to the program’s eligibility provisions.",
      "example": "Do not call the 240-day no-health-review period the final application deadline.",
      "topics": [
        "3.6.07"
      ]
    }
  ],
